Z||000014||fierosound||03-21-2001||12:19 AM||||How deep your subwoofers are will determine how deep the boxes will need to<BR>be, regardless of whether you cut into the firewall or not. I've done some<BR>more research and have found that some shallow depth subs are now made.<BR>These are high-end high Sound Quality subs. Be warned, you won't find these<BR>on sale at WalMart!!<P>Illusion Audio speakers and subwoofers - subs as shallow as 2.9" <BR><A HREF="http://www.illusionaudio.com/illusion/Driversx.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.illusionaudio.com/illusion/Driversx.html</A> <BR>Illusion Audio's trademark is placing the motor assembly (magnet & voice<BR>coil) in FRONT of the speaker cone instead of behind it on mid-bass drivers<BR>and subwoofers. This allows for increased flexibility and the option for<BR>large diameter speakers in shallow depth locations.<P>Phase Linear S1 10" subs have shallow mounting depth - as low as 3" (80mm)<BR> <A HREF="http://www.phase-linear.com/html/aliante_10si_data.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.phase-linear.com/html/aliante_10si_data.html</A> <P>Morel has very shallow 6" and 8" woofers at 2.5" <A HREF="http://www.morelusa.com/prices/ren.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.morelusa.com/prices/ren.html</A> <P>These come highly recommended by the guys at the <A HREF="http://www.sounddomain.com" TARGET=_blank>www.sounddomain.com</A> forums...<P>In my setup I am using 10" OZ Audio 250L subs - about 4.5" deep.<BR>How to build the behind the seat sub-boxes - check here <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003613.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/003613.html</A> <BR>More pics - other people's progress here <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003006.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003006.html</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||normalicy||03-21-2001||01:30 AM||||All I can say is that I've installed in alot of Fiero's and unless you really really need those subs, you'd better invest in some tens (or even 8s).  Just think, even when you get the subs installed, you still have to find a place to mount the amp.  I don't recommend either compartments since both tend to get wet.<P>However, if you just have to have those 12s, then the most stealth way (and comfortable) to do it is ditch the spare and build a bandpass with the port going into the cab (if you're writing this post though, then I sorta doubt that you're up to the ammount of work this involves, heck, I won't even do it).  <P>If that's a little too extreme, then cutting the fire wall isn't such a bad idea.  Just check behind before you cut.  If those subs need more than 1.5 cubic feet of air each, then you'll have to get creative with the boxes AND cut the firewall (like pull the carpet behind the seats, cut a hole just big enough for the magnet to fit through with about an extra half inch clearance, set up some sides and a top to the box taking up the entire area up to the rear deck, whip out the fiberglass,start laying and build a little cubby for the magnet, make a pretty top, and mount subs, hope that you left room for the amp.<P>If that's a little much, just get a couple 6" bazookas and live with it.<P>That's probably more than you want to know.<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
